In the sticky heat of Stranglethorn Vale, Pip Sprocketsprocket, a gnome warlock, trudged through the jungle with his succubus, Velvet. “Why can’t I just use the voidwalker like everyone else?” he muttered, swatting a mosquito. Velvet smirked, vanishing into stealth. A panther stalked nearby. Before it could pounce, Velvet reappeared, charming it. The panther stood docile as Pip drained its life. “See?” she purred. “I’m more than just a distraction.” Pip grinned, realizing her seduction skill made crowd control effortless—but most warlocks never learned to use it properly.

The answer: Most warlocks use voidwalkers because the succubus’s Seduction ability is a crowd control spell that breaks on damage, requiring careful micro-management. Many players find it easier to just let the voidwalker tank, even though Seduction can make leveling safer and more efficient. [end of text]


